Since $90^3=729,\!000$, $\text{AB}$ is greater than 90.  Therefore, $\text{A}=9$.  Since the ones digit of $\text{AB}^3$ is 3, $\text{AB}$ must be odd.  The ones digit of $\text{AB}^3$ is the same as the ones digit of $\text{B}^3$, so we look at the ones digits of the cubes of the odd digits. \[
\begin{array}{c}
\text{The ones digit of }1^3 \text{ is } 1. \\ \text{The ones digit of }3^3 \text{ is } 7. \\ \text{The ones digit of }5^3 \text{ is } 5. \\ \text{The ones digit of }7^3 \text{ is } 3. \\ \text{The ones digit of }9^3 \text{ is } 9.
\end{array}
\] Only $7^3$ has a ones digit of 3, so $\text{B}=7$.  Therefore, $\text{A}+\text{B}=9+7=\boxed{16}$.

A $90^\circ$ rotation around the origin in the counter-clockwise direction corresponds to multiplication by $\operatorname{cis} 90^\circ = i.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.5 cm);

draw((-3,0)--(8,0));
draw((0,-1)--(0,8));
draw((0,0)--(7,2),dashed);
draw((0,0)--(-2,7),dashed);

dot("$7 + 2i$", (7,2), E);
dot("$-2 + 7i$", (-2,7), N);
[/asy]

Thus, the image of $7 + 2i$ is $i(7 + 2i) = \boxed{-2 + 7i}.$

By the Binomial Theorem,
\begin{align*}
(z + i)^4 &= z^4 + 4z^3 i + 6z^2 i^2 + 4zi^3 + 1 \\
&= z^4 + 4iz^3 - 6z^2 - 4iz + 1.
\end{align*}So, if $z^4 + 4z^3 i - 6z^2 - 4zi - i = 0,$ then
\[(z + i)^4 = z^4 + 4iz^3 - 6z^2 - 4iz + 1 = 1 + i.\]Let $w = z + i,$ so $w^4 = 1 + i.$  (If we plot the solutions $w$ in the complex plane, we obtain the same area as from the solutions $z$ in the complex plane, because the substitution $w = z + i$ simply translates the polygon.)

If $w^4 = 1 + i,$ then
\[(wi)^4 = w^4 i^4 = w^4 = 1 + i.\]Thus, if $w$ is a solution, then so are $iw,$ $i^2 w = -w,$ and $i^3 w = -iw,$ which form a square in the complex plane.

[asy]
unitsize(2 cm);

pair A, B, C, D;

A = 2^(1/8)*dir(45/4);
B = 2^(1/8)*dir(45/4 + 90);
C = 2^(1/8)*dir(45/4 + 180);
D = 2^(1/8)*dir(45/4 + 270);

draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
draw((-1.5,0)--(1.5,0));
draw((0,-1.5)--(0,1.5));

dot("$w$", A, E);
dot("$iw$", B, N);
dot("$-w$", C, W);
dot("$-iw$", D, S);
[/asy]

From the equation $w^4 = 1 + i,$ $|w^4| = |1 + i|.$  Then $|w|^4 = \sqrt{2},$ so $|w| = 2^{1/8}.$  Therefore, the side length of the square is
\[|w - iw| = |w||1 - i| = 2^{1/8} \sqrt{2} = 2^{5/8},\]so the area of the square is $(2^{5/8})^2 = 2^{5/4}.$  The final answer is $5 + 4 + 2 = \boxed{11}.$

A $90^\circ$ rotation in the clockwise direction corresponds to multiplication by $\operatorname{cis} (-90^\circ) = -i.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair O = (-4,-5), A = (3,0), B = rotate(-90,O)*(A);

draw(O--A,dashed);
draw(O--B,dashed);

dot("$3$", A, NE);
dot("$1 - 12i$", B, SE);
dot("$-4 - 5i$", O, W);
[/asy]

Let $z$ be the image of $3$ under the rotation.  Since center of the rotation is $-4 - 5i,$
\[z - (-4 - 5i) = (-i)(3 - (-4 - 5i)).\]Solving, we find $z = \boxed{1 - 12i}.$

